WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.088
- Well, what university are
you going to sign up for?

00:00:03.088 --> 00:00:04.097
What are you going to do?

00:00:04.097 --> 00:00:05.469
And I was like, "Oh I'm
going to be an actor."

00:00:05.469 --> 00:00:07.120
And she's like, "Well, okay,
but what's your backup?"

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.439
And I didn't really have one,

00:00:08.439 --> 00:00:10.153
there was nothing else
I really wanted to do.

00:00:10.153 --> 00:00:11.426
That's the first thing Ron said to me,

00:00:11.426 --> 00:00:12.835
"You know, Thor's not going to fit into

00:00:12.835 --> 00:00:14.246
a formula one car."

00:00:14.246 --> 00:00:15.979
(audience laughs)

00:00:15.979 --> 00:00:17.937
"I'll lose weight. I'll lose
weight. I'll lose weight."

00:00:17.937 --> 00:00:19.456
You know, what usually scares you the most

00:00:19.456 --> 00:00:21.008
is what doesn't actually exist, you know?

00:00:21.008 --> 00:00:23.448
Even amongst doing it,
it's actually not so bad.

00:00:23.448 --> 00:00:25.370
- Hello Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:27.435
My one word is Believe.

00:00:27.435 --> 00:00:29.341
And I believe in people

00:00:29.341 --> 00:00:30.442
more than they believe in themselves.

00:00:30.442 --> 00:00:32.784
And my sincere hope is
that if you see in yourself

00:00:32.784 --> 00:00:36.776
what I see in you, you'll be
able to change the planet.

00:00:36.776 --> 00:00:38.432
So, to help you on your journey today,

00:00:38.432 --> 00:00:40.225
we're going to learn from
actor Chris Hemsworth

00:00:40.225 --> 00:00:43.089
and my take on his Top
Ten Rules for Success.

00:00:43.089 --> 00:00:44.714
Rule number two is my personal favorite

00:00:44.714 --> 00:00:47.173
and I'd love to know which
one you guys liked the best.

00:00:47.173 --> 00:00:48.599
Also as you're watching,

00:00:48.599 --> 00:00:50.349
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:52.024
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:52.024 --> 00:00:53.624
and put quotes around it
so other people can be

00:00:53.624 --> 00:00:55.285
inspired by it as well.

00:00:55.285 --> 00:00:56.932
And if you leave it within
the first couple hours

00:00:56.932 --> 00:00:58.245
of this video going live,

00:00:58.245 --> 00:01:02.328
you have a chance to win
one of two daily prizes.

00:01:14.094 --> 00:01:16.687
- Do you remember the first
actor who inspired you?

00:01:16.687 --> 00:01:17.994
Someone you saw on screen?

00:01:17.994 --> 00:01:20.541
- I remember watching
Brad Pitt a lot as a kid

00:01:20.541 --> 00:01:23.442
and just loving sort of charisma
or whatever he was doing.

00:01:23.442 --> 00:01:25.172
The type of films he was making.

00:01:25.172 --> 00:01:26.727
And prior to even knowing
I wanted to be an actor

00:01:26.727 --> 00:01:28.908
it was things like NeverEnding
Story and Princess Bride.

00:01:28.908 --> 00:01:30.957
And those kind of films that I just loved.

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:32.631
- Do you remember the first time

00:01:32.631 --> 00:01:34.173
you thought about moving over here?

00:01:34.173 --> 00:01:35.634
- Yeah, it was when I was finishing school

00:01:35.634 --> 00:01:37.421
and talking to you know,

00:01:37.421 --> 00:01:39.291
the careers advisor at school about,

00:01:39.291 --> 00:01:40.967
"Okay, well what
university are you going to

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:42.472
sign up for, what are you going to do?"

00:01:42.472 --> 00:01:43.882
And I was like, "Oh I'm
going to be an actor."

00:01:43.882 --> 00:01:45.707
And she's like, "Well, okay,
but what's your backup?"

00:01:45.707 --> 00:01:47.487
And I didn't really have one.

00:01:47.487 --> 00:01:49.166
There was nothing else
I really wanted to do.

00:01:49.166 --> 00:01:51.675
And, and so, you know?

00:01:51.675 --> 00:01:53.599
I put my head down and went for it.

00:01:53.599 --> 00:01:56.211
Downey, very early on
when I got cast as Thor,

00:01:56.211 --> 00:01:57.630
they brought me onto the set of Iron Man,

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.493
and I remember walking on, you know,

00:01:59.493 --> 00:02:01.431
in just, track suit pants and a T-shirt

00:02:01.431 --> 00:02:04.172
and seeing Downey there
in the Iron Man costume

00:02:04.172 --> 00:02:06.609
and knowing that Avengers was coming up

00:02:06.609 --> 00:02:08.197
and just thinking,

00:02:08.197 --> 00:02:10.123
"How am I going to pull Thor off?

00:02:10.123 --> 00:02:12.182
"And how the hell am I going
to stand opposite of this guy?

00:02:12.182 --> 00:02:14.299
"And do this?" You know?

00:02:14.299 --> 00:02:17.153
But he was, you know, he was great.

00:02:17.153 --> 00:02:18.443
He gave me some great advice about

00:02:18.443 --> 00:02:21.215
what an opportunity that
we were all involved in.

00:02:21.215 --> 00:02:23.194
It wasn't directed you know, at me.

00:02:23.194 --> 00:02:25.622
It was at himself, he was
talking the group, you know.

00:02:25.622 --> 00:02:27.934
He knew Captain America
was coming, Thor, Iron Man

00:02:27.934 --> 00:02:30.718
and he said, "You know, we're
part of something special.

00:02:30.718 --> 00:02:32.571
"And this is, you know, going to be

00:02:32.571 --> 00:02:34.201
"a hell of a lot of fun"

00:02:34.201 --> 00:02:38.917
And, and that's it you
know, I think you got to ...

00:02:38.917 --> 00:02:40.965
You got to enjoy the process, you know?

00:02:40.965 --> 00:02:43.350
It's um, if you focus too much on sort of,

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.170
"What's the result of it all?"

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:48.093
You know, you're not
right in the middle of it

00:02:48.093 --> 00:02:50.219
you know, this is going to sound cliche,

00:02:50.219 --> 00:02:51.851
but in the moment of it all, you know?

00:02:51.851 --> 00:02:55.109
And um, he kind of, that was
a nice kind of reminder of it.

00:02:55.109 --> 00:02:57.431
And I tried to kind of keep
that with everything I do,

00:02:57.431 --> 00:02:58.961
it's just ...

00:02:58.961 --> 00:03:00.754
As an actor you don't control

00:03:00.754 --> 00:03:02.222
the spectrum.

00:03:02.222 --> 00:03:04.845
You know, that's the directors
and producers and stuff.

00:03:04.845 --> 00:03:07.592
If you do theater, sure you
come out and do your thing but,

00:03:07.592 --> 00:03:10.052
on film, on film you
do 10 different takes.

00:03:10.052 --> 00:03:12.270
And you know, across how
ever many days you know,

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.261
60 days or whatever.

00:03:14.261 --> 00:03:17.145
There's 40 or 50 different
versions of Thor.

00:03:17.145 --> 00:03:19.672
You know, that Kenneth
Branagh caught together

00:03:19.672 --> 00:03:20.901
in the first film.

00:03:20.901 --> 00:03:23.443
You know, like, whether
you liked it or not that,

00:03:23.443 --> 00:03:25.801
you know, that was a version
that was put together

00:03:25.801 --> 00:03:27.638
by a lot of other people, you know?

00:03:27.638 --> 00:03:30.172
And that was something kind of satisfy--

00:03:30.172 --> 00:03:32.606
or it takes the weight off in thinking

00:03:32.606 --> 00:03:35.130
"You know what, I can't control this.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:36.852
"No matter how much I think I can.

00:03:36.852 --> 00:03:38.219
"Or I try to."

00:03:38.219 --> 00:03:41.318
Um, there's some comfort in
kind of, letting go with it.

00:03:41.318 --> 00:03:43.079
And especially walking
onto a set like this,

00:03:43.079 --> 00:03:44.918
with an ensemble like that,

00:03:44.918 --> 00:03:47.860
it's like, "Good, we can
share this weight, you know."

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.250
Although the finger gets
pointed at individuals,

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:53.053
whether director, writer, actor, whatever

00:03:53.053 --> 00:03:55.371
it's the same effort,
work or fail you know.

00:03:55.371 --> 00:03:57.048
- Our first time meet,

00:03:57.048 --> 00:03:59.987
I saw him trying to get
into a formula three car.

00:03:59.987 --> 00:04:02.040
I cracked up, I thought,

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.040
"This is Thor trying to get into..."

00:04:08.249 --> 00:04:09.517
- That's what Ron says.

00:04:09.517 --> 00:04:10.807
The first thing Ron said to me,

00:04:10.807 --> 00:04:13.474
"You know, Thor's not going
to fit in a formula 1 car."

00:04:13.474 --> 00:04:15.573
(audience laughs)

00:04:15.573 --> 00:04:18.665
"I'll lost weight, I'll lose
weight, I'll lose weight."

00:04:18.665 --> 00:04:20.927
- Did they have to build a car for you?

00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:22.661
- Uh, we had to pull the
seat out a couple of times

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:24.306
and I had to sit on the chassis

00:04:24.306 --> 00:04:26.350
and the metal piping on the ...

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:29.597
but James, James was
the same height as me.

00:04:29.597 --> 00:04:30.430
- Really?

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:32.138
- He was one of the
tallest Formula 1 drivers.

00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:35.287
And had a hell of time,
getting in the car and um,

00:04:35.287 --> 00:04:37.754
the weight I got down
to was similar to him

00:04:37.754 --> 00:04:39.982
so I could, I understood his feelings.

00:04:39.982 --> 00:04:41.247
- [Interviewer] The
biggest challenge in your

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:43.580
process of characterization.

00:04:45.249 --> 00:04:47.099
- I'm sure there's, beside
me, what Daniel was saying

00:04:47.099 --> 00:04:50.227
uh, as far as uh, you're
playing a real person.

00:04:50.227 --> 00:04:52.342
And it's this iconic figure and

00:04:52.342 --> 00:04:56.272
it becomes the, a whole
lot of responsibility

00:04:56.272 --> 00:04:59.292
and weight and you
know, his family members

00:04:59.292 --> 00:05:02.747
and what have you that
you want to be respectful

00:05:02.747 --> 00:05:05.590
and you know, do it justice.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:08.636
I've made the mistake of
going to a pre-kickoff party

00:05:08.636 --> 00:05:11.929
for the film with a whole bunch of drivers

00:05:11.929 --> 00:05:13.949
and people from the
industry who knew these guys

00:05:13.949 --> 00:05:15.868
and I thought, "Oh this'll be great.

00:05:15.868 --> 00:05:17.430
"You know, I'll get some good advice."

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.607
And, within like, five minutes
of being there I was like,

00:05:19.607 --> 00:05:21.031
"What am I doing here?"

00:05:21.031 --> 00:05:22.327
because everyone was like,

00:05:22.327 --> 00:05:24.044
"Oh, you know he doesn't look like this."

00:05:24.044 --> 00:05:25.820
And you know, "He speaks like this."

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:27.110
And, "What are you going
to do with the accent?"

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:28.517
And, "How are you going to walk?"

00:05:28.517 --> 00:05:31.518
And it was like, "Oh my God,
these opinions." You know?

00:05:31.518 --> 00:05:34.887
And um, but then I adopted the thing of,

00:05:34.887 --> 00:05:37.932
you know what, just like he
approached life and driving

00:05:37.932 --> 00:05:39.759
as did Niki, which was do it your way

00:05:39.759 --> 00:05:41.883
and disregard the, you know,

00:05:41.883 --> 00:05:43.923
sort of exterior opinions out there.

00:05:43.923 --> 00:05:46.101
- [Interviewer] Thor
seems almost fearless.

00:05:46.101 --> 00:05:48.134
Is there anything that scares you?

00:05:48.134 --> 00:05:49.236
Like, in your life?

00:05:49.236 --> 00:05:50.319
- In my life?

00:05:51.808 --> 00:05:53.641
Look, a lot of things.

00:05:54.927 --> 00:05:56.885
Mainly uh, I think most of us

00:05:56.885 --> 00:05:58.552
our mind is sort of,

00:05:59.899 --> 00:06:02.622
you know, convinces us things
are worse than they are.

00:06:02.622 --> 00:06:03.918
Or better than they are,

00:06:03.918 --> 00:06:05.753
it sets us up for a fall
constantly you know?

00:06:05.753 --> 00:06:08.905
And this business, I think
about that a lot, you know?

00:06:08.905 --> 00:06:10.829
About what's real, what's not

00:06:10.829 --> 00:06:12.997
and what exactly am I doing

00:06:12.997 --> 00:06:14.645
and what do I aspire to be and why,

00:06:14.645 --> 00:06:16.542
and you know, all those sort of questions.

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.729
And I always come back to
just simplifying things.

00:06:18.729 --> 00:06:20.826
You know, just sort of day by day.

00:06:20.826 --> 00:06:22.638
You know, usually what scares you the most

00:06:22.638 --> 00:06:24.315
is what doesn't actually exist.

00:06:24.315 --> 00:06:26.810
Even amongst doing it,
it's actually not so bad.

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:28.704
You know, like that coming out here.

00:06:28.704 --> 00:06:31.500
Before hand it was like, "Oh
my god, oh my god, oh my god."

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.161
But you get out here and you go, "Okay.

00:06:33.161 --> 00:06:35.202
"I'm alive. I lived."

00:06:35.202 --> 00:06:36.285
And um, yeah.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:40.169
- Grew up near Melbourne, Australia,

00:06:40.169 --> 00:06:42.637
in an aboriginal uh, community, huh?

00:06:42.637 --> 00:06:43.941
- I did yeah, I grew up in Melbourne

00:06:43.941 --> 00:06:46.120
and then moved to uh, Northern Territory,

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:47.451
in an aboriginal community.

00:06:47.451 --> 00:06:49.663
There were buffalo and crocodiles

00:06:49.663 --> 00:06:51.937
and it was a rather, you
know, unusual childhood

00:06:51.937 --> 00:06:53.931
compared to the rest of the--

00:06:53.931 --> 00:06:56.037
- Did your parents work,
move you there, or?

00:06:56.037 --> 00:06:58.111
- Yeah, my parents worked
on a cattle station

00:06:58.111 --> 00:06:59.416
with my aunt and uncle.

00:06:59.416 --> 00:07:00.249
- [Kelly] I see.

00:07:00.249 --> 00:07:01.724
- And so, we as kids, went up and just--

00:07:01.724 --> 00:07:02.752
- [Regis] Well, that must
have been fun as a kid.

00:07:02.752 --> 00:07:03.777
- It was great, yeah.

00:07:03.777 --> 00:07:05.555
It was my introduction to life.

00:07:05.555 --> 00:07:08.506
And it was vivid and as
exciting as it could have been.

00:07:08.506 --> 00:07:10.077
- How does somebody start a career like,

00:07:10.077 --> 00:07:11.870
like what you've accomplished,

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:13.567
in a community like that?

00:07:13.567 --> 00:07:15.327
- Uh, you know, we were back and forth.

00:07:15.327 --> 00:07:16.894
We didn't live there the whole time

00:07:16.894 --> 00:07:18.528
but I think part of living there

00:07:18.528 --> 00:07:20.380
you know, sparked some

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:21.827
you know, imagination.

00:07:21.827 --> 00:07:24.731
And my dad read many books to us as kids

00:07:24.731 --> 00:07:27.658
and always had a love for
story telling and films

00:07:27.658 --> 00:07:28.575
and fantasy

00:07:29.744 --> 00:07:31.874
and I think that was sort
of the beginning of it all.

00:07:31.874 --> 00:07:34.004
And then it wasn't until I
finished school I thought,

00:07:34.004 --> 00:07:36.794
"This looks like fun, I could
do this maybe as a career."

00:07:36.794 --> 00:07:38.631
- So, you started acting in Australia?

00:07:38.631 --> 00:07:39.683
- Yeah. I tried to.

00:07:39.683 --> 00:07:41.068
I spent a few years on building sites

00:07:41.068 --> 00:07:44.074
and horticultural laboring and you know?

00:07:44.074 --> 00:07:46.330
It allowed me to take a
day off if I needed to.

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:49.996
- But then JJ Abrams saw
you as a part of Star Trek?

00:07:49.996 --> 00:07:51.468
- Yeah.
- And brought you over?

00:07:51.468 --> 00:07:52.521
- He did. Yeah.

00:07:52.521 --> 00:07:54.164
I was in America at the time and just shot

00:07:54.164 --> 00:07:57.227
a little independent film and came back

00:07:57.227 --> 00:07:58.294
and it was JJ.

00:07:58.294 --> 00:08:00.254
It was on a Friday night in his office

00:08:00.254 --> 00:08:03.419
across his desk and he said,
and we read the scene, he said,

00:08:03.419 --> 00:08:05.190
"That's great, you'll start on Monday."

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:06.177
And that was--

00:08:06.177 --> 00:08:08.844
- Wow.
- The Starship Enterprise.

00:08:19.691 --> 00:08:22.048
In Mark Ruffalo, you don't see the Hulk.

00:08:22.048 --> 00:08:24.822
Which, is I think, the
perfect sort of contrast

00:08:24.822 --> 00:08:27.074
because it's that sort of vulnerability

00:08:27.074 --> 00:08:30.663
and that you know, um
fear of what's to come

00:08:30.663 --> 00:08:32.310
if he does turn into that guy.

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:33.952
He doesn't necessarily like
who that guy is, you know.

00:08:33.952 --> 00:08:35.712
But it's just a good bunch, you know.

00:08:35.712 --> 00:08:37.275
I mean, Robert is uh,

00:08:37.275 --> 00:08:39.164
is an actor and you know,

00:08:39.164 --> 00:08:40.949
everything that he's done is incredible

00:08:40.949 --> 00:08:42.735
and as is all of this cast

00:08:42.735 --> 00:08:44.281
and so watching all of them work and

00:08:44.281 --> 00:08:45.518
their different styles

00:08:45.518 --> 00:08:47.836
and getting everyone
together in one big scene,

00:08:47.836 --> 00:08:49.435
it's been really exciting.

00:08:49.435 --> 00:08:50.379
- [Interviewer] Can you
tell me about working with

00:08:50.379 --> 00:08:51.711
Kenneth Branagh, on this?

00:08:51.711 --> 00:08:54.060
- Yeah, he's incredible.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:56.073
Um, he was the first play I
ever did, or acting piece,

00:08:56.073 --> 00:08:57.708
was "Much Ado About Nothing."

00:08:57.708 --> 00:08:59.465
Which, you know, we all studied his film

00:08:59.465 --> 00:09:01.556
over and over again.

00:09:01.556 --> 00:09:04.052
So, to come full circle
and work with him now

00:09:04.052 --> 00:09:05.586
is incredible.

00:09:05.586 --> 00:09:07.303
Ken's one of the people
I've never been challenged

00:09:07.303 --> 00:09:10.700
with changing things, you
know, doing it different.

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:13.049
Like, "Oh we got that, now try this."

00:09:13.049 --> 00:09:15.183
And it really gets you
out of that sort of,

00:09:15.183 --> 00:09:17.337
you know, bad habit of saying,

00:09:17.337 --> 00:09:19.123
"Oh no, my character only does this."

00:09:19.123 --> 00:09:21.110
And I think, when you limit
yourself like that you,

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:23.049
limit your possibilities.

00:09:23.049 --> 00:09:25.545
Ken was all about experimenting

00:09:25.545 --> 00:09:26.990
and just going for it

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:28.256
and taking that risk

00:09:28.256 --> 00:09:30.189
and, it worked sometimes
and sometimes it doesn't

00:09:30.189 --> 00:09:32.410
and you know, that's part of the journey.

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:34.335
- Hard to actually get the muscle bulk on?

00:09:34.335 --> 00:09:36.453
- It was, the eating was
probably the most difficult part.

00:09:36.453 --> 00:09:38.423
It was, it wasn't the fun
kind of eating either.

00:09:38.423 --> 00:09:39.932
It was copious amounts of chicken breast

00:09:39.932 --> 00:09:42.560
and various animal
proteins and what have you.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:45.109
(men speaking over one another)

00:09:45.109 --> 00:09:46.523
- [Female interviewer]
He reckons it's CGI.

00:09:46.523 --> 00:09:48.050
- I wish it was that easy.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:49.249
- Is that real?

00:09:49.249 --> 00:09:51.017
- Yeah, I wouldn't have
spent 6 months in the gym.

00:09:51.017 --> 00:09:52.616
It was, I read the comic books

00:09:52.616 --> 00:09:54.708
and noticed the character
was 700 kilograms,

